export default [
{slug:'print-qr-code-quiet-zone-test',title:'Make a QR code that still scans after printing',category:'documents',summary:'Keep the quiet zone, choose a clear destination and test the actual printed result before distributing it.',tools:['qr-code','qr-reader','business-card'],sources:['qrBorder','qrCorrection','formats'],sections:[
{title:'Start with the destination, then style it',paragraphs:['A useful QR code gives a scanner exactly the information the visitor expects. For a website, use the full HTTPS address and check that it opens the intended page. A static code stores that address directly. Changing a page at the same address is different from changing the destination encoded in an already printed code.','Nepocore does not add a redirect or tracking service. That keeps the destination visible and avoids a dependency on a separate short-link account. It also means a typo remains a typo in every printed copy. Read the entered text carefully and test the decoded value before adding decorative colors or a logo.']},
{title:'Protect the empty border',paragraphs:['The quiet zone is the clear area around the square. DENSO WAVE specifies four modules around a standard QR code. A module is one grid unit, not a fixed number of millimetres. Keep that border when placing the SVG or PNG into a card, poster or slide. Cropping right to the dark squares removes part of the design needed for detection.','Use a light, plain background and a dark foreground with clear separation. A photograph behind the code or a decorative frame touching the grid can interfere with scanning. A text contrast score is useful for text design, but is not a QR-scanning certification.']},
{title:'Test density and decoration together',paragraphs:['Longer content generally makes a denser grid. At the same printed width, each module then becomes smaller. A short destination can therefore be easier to print at a useful size, but do not sacrifice trust by choosing a link service the reader cannot verify.','Error correction helps recover codewords; its advertised percentages are not a guarantee that a logo can cover the same percentage of the image. A logo can damage important patterns. Treat logos, rounded styles and unusual colors as extra variables that require real scanning tests.'],list:['Generate the code with its quiet zone intact.','Download SVG for a layout that needs scaling, or a sufficiently large PNG.','Read the export with QR reader and verify the complete decoded value.','Print a sample at the final physical size.','Scan the printed sample with more than one phone where possible.']},
{title:'Test the context people will actually use',paragraphs:['Scan from the distance, lighting and angle expected in use. A code beside a counter is different from one on a wall across a room. Glossy paper, folds and poor printer output can change a result that worked perfectly on screen. Keep essential instructions or a readable web address nearby so the QR code is not the only route.','Before a large print run, confirm that the destination is public, mobile-friendly and stable. Scanning a code does not establish that a site is safe. Readers should still review the displayed destination before opening it, and a QR reader should not automatically navigate to an untrusted address.']}
]},
{slug:'export-pdf-pages-jpg-png',title:'Export PDF pages as JPG or PNG without losing track of resolution',category:'documents',summary:'Choose page ranges and pixel density, then decide between a single image and a ZIP of page images.',tools:['pdf-to-images','pdf-extract','image-compressor'],sources:['pdfExamples','formats'],sections:[
{title:'Decide why you need an image',paragraphs:['A page image is useful for a preview, a presentation or an application that cannot display PDF. It is less useful when the recipient needs selectable text, accessible structure, form fields or clickable links. Rasterizing a page turns its visible appearance into pixels and leaves those PDF features behind. Keep the source document.','Use PDF to JPG / PNG to choose individual pages or ranges. For example, 3, 1-2 exports page three followed by pages one and two. Page numbers refer to positions in the file, which may differ from printed page labels such as an introduction numbered with Roman numerals. Check the preview and filenames after export.']},
{title:'Relate DPI to page dimensions',paragraphs:['PDF page dimensions are measured in points, with 72 points per inch. An A4 page is approximately 8.27 × 11.69 inches. At 150 pixels per inch, its rendered image is about 1240 × 1754 pixels. At 300, both dimensions double and the pixel count becomes about four times larger.','Higher density can improve small text, but it also increases memory and output size. The tool caps a rendered page at eight million pixels. Very large source pages may therefore export below the requested nominal resolution. Review the actual dimensions reported for the output rather than relying only on the DPI input.']},
{title:'Choose format and range deliberately',paragraphs:['JPEG is often convenient for scanned photographs and smaller sharing copies. PNG preserves sharp pixel boundaries without lossy JPEG artifacts and can suit diagrams or screenshots, but may create larger files. Compare the actual results for your document.'],list:['Choose an unprotected PDF no larger than the displayed limit.','Confirm the filename and page count in the upload status.','Enter a small page selection first.','Set JPEG or PNG and a suitable resolution.','Export one image, or download the ZIP when several pages are selected.'],paragraphsAfter:['The operation renders pages sequentially and limits each export to 30 selected pages. A large batch also has a combined output limit. If the operation is too large for your device, use fewer pages or a lower resolution. Closing the tool cancels its active document work.']},
{title:'Check before replacing or sharing anything',paragraphs:['Open the downloaded images. Inspect the smallest important text, page orientation, margins and any light-colored marks. ZIP files use numbered page names, which makes it easier to preserve the selected order in another application. Do not assume a successful download proves the page content is readable.','A screenshot of a PDF viewer may include interface buttons, a cursor or an incomplete page. Direct page rendering avoids those incidental elements. It still does not redact hidden source information in the original PDF: if you need to share only the rendered images, share those new files and keep the original separate. Use an appropriate redaction workflow for sensitive source documents.']}
]},
{slug:'compress-pdf-structure-or-image-copy',title:'PDF compression: compare a structure rewrite with an image-based copy',category:'documents',summary:'Understand why a PDF can get larger, and which features an image-based export removes.',tools:['pdf-compressor','pdf-extract','pdf-to-text'],sources:['pdfExamples','pdfText'],sections:[
{title:'A PDF can contain very different things',paragraphs:['A short PDF may contain compact vector text, huge photographs, embedded fonts or a mixture of scans and interactive elements. File size alone does not identify the cause. A document made from a few already optimized pages may have little room to shrink. Another may be dominated by unnecessarily large scans.','The compressor offers two distinct operations. A structure rewrite saves a new PDF without intentionally turning each page into a picture. An image-based copy renders each page and embeds JPEG images at a chosen resolution and quality. These operations have different tradeoffs and should be compared with the original.']},
{title:'Try the less destructive option first',paragraphs:['Choose Structure rewrite and create a copy. Compare the reported input and output bytes. If the result is larger, keep the original when size is the only goal. A rewrite is not a promise to optimize every internal stream, remove every unused resource or preserve a digital signature. Rewriting bytes can invalidate signatures.','Open the new copy and inspect page count, orientation and important content. Test any features you need rather than assuming all links, forms or annotations survived the processing library’s rewrite. Keep the untouched source until the recipient has accepted the document.']},
{title:'Use image-based output for an appropriate scan',paragraphs:['A photo-heavy scan may become smaller with a moderate raster resolution and JPEG quality. Start with a small representative document. For an A4 page, 120 pixels per inch gives roughly 992 × 1403 pixels; that may suit reading large print, while small text can require more. There is no single correct setting for all documents.','The image-based copy removes selectable text, links, forms and annotations as interactive PDF features. Visible marks can become part of the page image, but they are no longer editable objects. Accessibility information and text search are not preserved. Do not use this mode when those features are required.'],list:['Keep an original and create a clearly named copy.','Start with moderate resolution and quality.','Read the smallest text in the exported file.','Compare actual bytes, rather than assuming compression succeeded.','Test the recipient’s size limit and document requirements.']},
{title:'Reduce scope when the document is too large',paragraphs:['The tool accepts a PDF up to 30 MB and supports up to 40 pages for image-based copying. It caps rendered page pixels to protect memory. For a larger source, extract only the necessary pages first or use a dedicated document workflow suited to the file.','A smaller PDF is not automatically a better document. If a form stops working, a signature becomes invalid or a table becomes unreadable, the size saving has defeated the purpose. Preserve a searchable master, use a lightweight sharing copy only where appropriate, and tell the recipient if the copy is image-based.']}
]},
{slug:'extract-pdf-text-versus-ocr',title:'Extract PDF text or use OCR? Start by checking the text layer',category:'documents',summary:'Avoid empty extraction results and understand why columns, scans and reading order need different treatment.',tools:['pdf-to-text','image-to-text','pdf-to-images'],sources:['pdfText','pdfExamples'],sections:[
{title:'A visible word is not always stored as text',paragraphs:['A PDF can show a page of writing while containing only a photograph of that page. Another PDF can contain actual text objects, or an invisible OCR text layer behind the scan. Looking at the page does not reliably distinguish these cases. Try selecting and copying a short sentence in a trusted PDF reader as a first check.','PDF text extractor reads the existing text layer. It does not recognize letters inside page images. When extraction returns only page separators or an empty result, that can indicate an image-only scan. The correct next operation is OCR, rather than repeating the same text extraction settings.']},
{title:'Extract a small sample first',paragraphs:['Choose an unprotected PDF, confirm its page count and enter a page range. Begin with one page containing typical content. A single-column paragraph is a useful starting point; a multi-column research page or complex form needs more careful review.','The tool adds page separators to help you trace the text back to the source. These are navigation aids, not original document content. Copy the result into an editor or download the TXT file, then compare names, dates, numbers and paragraph order with the actual PDF.'],list:['Check whether text can be selected in the source.','Extract one or two representative pages.','Compare reading order and line breaks.','Expand the range only after that sample looks useful.','Keep the PDF alongside the text while proofreading.']},
{title:'Expect layout to change',paragraphs:['PDF stores drawing and text-positioning instructions rather than a guaranteed stream of paragraphs. A line of text may be split into several objects. Columns, footnotes, tables and headers can therefore appear in an unexpected order when extracted. A TXT export cannot preserve the original page design.','For a two-column page, read down the left column in the original and compare the extracted sequence. If the result jumps between columns, manually reorganize it or use a specialized document-layout workflow. For a table, confirm each value stays associated with the correct label before importing it into a spreadsheet.']},
{title:'Use OCR for page images and verify its guesses',paragraphs:['For a scan, render the needed PDF page as a clear image, then use Image to text. Choose the relevant supported language and inspect the OCR output. Blur, skew, handwriting and decorative type can produce errors. A larger image can help only when the source actually contains the detail.','The extractor supports up to 100 selected pages per operation, with file and device-memory limits. It processes text locally, but that does not make an unreviewed transcript reliable. Searchable text is convenient for notes and accessibility workflows; it still needs human checking before quotations, records or important numerical decisions. Store the source and corrected transcript as separate files so later readers know which is authoritative.']}
]},
{slug:'word-search-custom-list-answer-key',title:'Build a word search from a list that actually fits',category:'learning',summary:'Choose a grid, useful vocabulary and an answer key, while handling words the generator cannot place.',tools:['word-search','kids-worksheets','text-formatter'],sources:['printing','page'],sections:[
{title:'Give the puzzle a learning purpose',paragraphs:['A word search can reinforce recognition of a focused vocabulary set. Start with a theme such as animals, classroom objects or terms from one lesson. Eight related words with a short follow-up activity can be more useful than thirty unrelated words chosen only to fill a page.','The generator uses English A–Z words. Remove spaces, punctuation and accents deliberately, and tell learners how multiword terms are represented. Avoid placing two answers that differ only by punctuation the generator removes. Review the normalized list so the printed word bank matches the actual hidden entries.']},
{title:'Match the grid to the longest word',paragraphs:['A word cannot fit in a straight line if it is longer than the chosen grid dimension. A 12-letter term needs at least 12 cells in its direction. Even when each word fits individually, a crowded group may not fit together. Word length, shared letters and allowed directions all affect placement.','Begin with a 12 × 12 grid for a short list of moderate-length words, then adjust. Forward horizontal and vertical directions are easier to explain than backward diagonals. Add directions only when they suit the intended learners. The seeded layout lets you regenerate the same set rather than relying on an unrepeatable random page.'],list:['Enter one theme-based word per line.','Choose the grid size and allowed directions.','Generate the puzzle and read any unplaced-word notice.','Increase the grid or shorten the list when needed.','Review the answer key before printing both pages.']},
{title:'Check the puzzle and the answer key together',paragraphs:['Do not silently remove a word that failed to place while leaving it in a learner’s task. Nepocore reports missing entries. Revise the set until the printed bank and grid match what you intend. The answer key is a practical verification tool, not just a teacher convenience.','Read each answer letter by letter in its marked position. Check that a backward word is allowed if one appears. Random filler may accidentally form other words; inspect the grid if that matters for the audience. A generated puzzle still benefits from a quick editorial review.']},
{title:'Make the printed activity readable',paragraphs:['Download the combined PDF for the puzzle and key, or export separate images. Match A4 or Letter settings to the paper and inspect print preview. The puzzle should have enough space for marking without hiding neighboring letters. Avoid printing a small screenshot of a full page.','Keep the answer key separate during the activity. Add a second task, such as defining three terms or using them in sentences, when the goal goes beyond recognition. The tool generates a layout; it does not assess reading level, vocabulary mastery or accessibility needs. Offer a large-print or simpler-direction version when a learner needs it.']}
]},
{slug:'crossword-words-clues-connected-layout',title:'Create a connected crossword with clear clues',category:'learning',summary:'Write fair answer–clue pairs, improve intersections and review unplaced words before handing out the puzzle.',tools:['crossword','word-search','text-formatter'],sources:['printing','page'],sections:[
{title:'Write the answers before generating the grid',paragraphs:['A useful classroom crossword begins with a small, coherent answer list. Choose terms learners have encountered and decide exactly which spelling belongs in the grid. The builder uses A–Z answers, so remove spaces deliberately and explain the convention for multiword phrases.','Write clues that point to one intended answer. A clue such as “an animal” is too broad; “a striped big cat” narrows the task. For a lesson review, use the lesson’s definitions without copying a published puzzle. Keep clues concise enough to read on the printed page.']},
{title:'Connected words need shared letters',paragraphs:['A connected layout cannot be built from arbitrary terms if they have no suitable intersections. The generator tries arrangements, but it cannot create shared letters that are absent. A missing entry is a signal to revise the vocabulary set or add a bridging word, rather than an error to ignore.','Suppose CAT, TIGER and RABBIT are in the list. They share several potential crossing letters, but their positions must still avoid conflicting neighboring cells. Short and long words together often give more flexibility than a group of equally long answers. Changing the layout seed can find a different arrangement, but does not guarantee that every set will fit.'],list:['Enter each answer with its clue in the displayed format.','Generate a layout and read the unplaced-entry notice.','Revise or add a bridging answer where needed.','Check the numbered Across and Down lists.','Print the puzzle, clue page and answer key as required.']},
{title:'Review fairness separately from fit',paragraphs:['A technically valid crossing is not enough. Read each clue without looking at its answer and consider whether the intended audience has enough information. Avoid clues that depend on an obscure fact unless that fact is part of the lesson. Check singular and plural forms, abbreviations and spelling variants.','Compare every numbered start cell with the clue list and key. Across and Down can share a start number, which is normal when both answers begin in the same square. Inspect the generated page at its final size; a dense grid with tiny numbers can be difficult to use even when the layout fits.']},
{title:'Use the output as an editable teaching resource',paragraphs:['The builder supports a bounded answer list and reports entries it cannot place. It is a layout aid, rather than a full publishing system or a guarantee of a newspaper-style symmetrical crossword. Keep your answer–clue text so you can revise it later.','Use PDF for the printable set and PNG or SVG for a page you want to place in another document. Keep the solution separate from the learner copy. If the objective is vocabulary practice rather than spelling recall, provide a word bank or use the word-search tool instead. Choose the activity that supports the lesson, and adapt it for learners who need larger text or a different response format.']}
]},
{slug:'sudoku-unique-solution-checker',title:'Use a Sudoku generator, checker and hint without guessing what they mean',category:'learning',summary:'Learn the difference between conflicts, wrong entries, uniqueness and clue-count difficulty.',tools:['sudoku','word-search','crossword'],sources:['printing'],sections:[
{title:'Keep the three constraints visible',paragraphs:['A standard nine-by-nine Sudoku uses the digits 1–9 once in every row, column and three-by-three box. The initial clues are fixed. A valid completed grid satisfies all three constraints, not just the rows. A repeated digit in a box can be easy to miss when two neighboring rows look correct on their own.','Nepocore generates a completed grid, removes clues and checks that the remaining puzzle has one solution. Uniqueness means there is one valid full completion. It does not mean a beginner will find the next step obvious or that the puzzle has a certified human-solving rating.']},
{title:'Treat difficulty as a starting point',paragraphs:['The difficulty choices target different numbers of clues. Fewer clues can increase the challenge, but clue count alone does not measure which techniques a person needs. Two puzzles with similar counts may feel quite different because the positions and relationships of the clues differ.','Use the seed or puzzle-set field to reproduce a puzzle. That is useful when a teacher and learner need to discuss the same grid, or when you want to print a second copy. Changing the set creates another layout, while the printed answer key provides a complete reference.']},
{title:'Use checking to learn from a specific mistake',paragraphs:['A conflict is a repeated digit in a row, column or box. An entry can also be wrong relative to the solution without immediately repeating a nearby digit. Read the checker’s message carefully instead of assuming that no visible duplicate proves the entry is correct.','When checking identifies a problem, revisit the reasoning that produced that digit. Was a candidate excluded by the wrong box? Did you read the wrong row? Remove uncertain entries before adding more. A hint can reveal one cell, but using it is more useful when you then examine why that cell fits.'],list:['Generate a puzzle and note its set and difficulty.','Fill only editable cells; keep the original clues intact.','Check after a small group of reasoned entries.','Use a hint when needed and review the related row, column and box.','Reset entries to start that puzzle again, or generate a new set.']},
{title:'Print a puzzle and keep the key separate',paragraphs:['The printable output includes a puzzle and a solution page. Review print preview and make sure cell borders and clue digits remain clear on the actual paper. Pencil marks need room, so a full-size grid may be more practical than fitting several tiny screenshots on one sheet.','A generated puzzle is recreational practice, not a cognitive assessment. Completion time does not diagnose ability, intelligence or health. If a particular grid is frustrating, choose an easier setting or a fresh set and focus on one technique at a time. The browser worker handles generation so the interface can stay responsive; device speed still affects how quickly uniqueness checks complete.']}
]}
];
